NAME
    ls - list directory contents

SYNOPSIS
    ls [-l] [-a] [path...]

DESCRIPTION
    Lists its operands, sorted by name (case-insensitive). With no operand,
    lists the current directory. File operands are listed together as a group;
    each directory operand has its contents listed, with a "name:" header when
    there is more than one operand. Files print plain; directories with a
    trailing /. The default layout is multi-column; dotfiles are hidden.

    Takes any number of operands, so shell wildcards work: `ls *.txt`.

OPTIONS
    -l    Long form, one entry per line: a permission string (drwxr-xr-x),
          owner uid and gid, size, and modified YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM. Mode and
          owner are real on ext2 (the one filesystem that stores them - e.g.
          lost+found shows drwx------ 0 0); FAT32/exFAT/proc cannot model an
          owner, so a conventional mode is shown and the owner is a dash. The
          time is shown where the filesystem records one (FAT32 does; others
          show a dash).
    -a    Include dotfiles, and the . and .. entries.

EXAMPLES
    ls
    ls -l /bin
    ls -l *.txt
    ls dir1 dir2

SEE ALSO
    tree, cat, stat
